 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the storage implementation. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, SQLite preparation, activation, and lookup all belong to this node. Each kiosk holds the active registry as a local embedded SQLite database. No registry lookup requires a service, credentials, or network connection. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of the storage choice.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Stream the verified newline-delimited JSON records into a newly created SQLite database in the registry storage directory. Store the canonical lookup serial as the primary key and store the snapshot date with the imported data. A duplicate canonical serial causes the import to fail.
+
+Prepare the database under a unique candidate name and run SQLite integrity validation before it becomes eligible for use. Keep the active database name in a small durable local activation record. After the candidate is complete and valid, atomically replace that record to point to the candidate, then make the candidate the database used by new lookups. A process-local read/write boundary ensures a lookup observes either the former active database or the replacement database, never a partial import. Candidate databases left by an interrupted or rejected import are not active and may be removed on a later import or application start.
+
+The active database and its activation record are the kiosk's local index. The source snapshot, candidate databases, and the active database are all local files; importing a snapshot neither contacts nor depends on an external directory or search service.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the SQLite primary key for the canonical serial. Return the active database's snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The database is opened read-only for lookup and contains only the fields required to construct the fixed registry result type.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup index | Embedded SQLite database on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Keeps all lookup data and execution on the kiosk, satisfying the offline operating model while providing indexed serial-number lookup for the full snapshot. |
+| Replacement activation | Build a separate candidate database, then atomically switch the durable active-database record | Rebuild the active database in place; expose candidate records during import | A failed or interrupted import leaves the known-good active database selectable, and readers have a complete snapshot for their whole lookup. |
+| Serial key | Normalized serial as the SQLite primary key | Raw serial key with application-side normalization; non-unique index | Applies the lookup contract consistently at import and query time and makes duplicate canonical serials rejectable. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
